# Merge and line-ending rules for it-ops-kb. Built-in git drivers only: nothing to configure per clone.
# After any merge or pull: python3 _tools/kbgit.py fix, then python3 _tools/tests.py.

# Text is stored and checked out with LF everywhere (Windows clones too).
* text=auto eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Append-only ledgers: union merge. When both branches add lines at the same place, git keeps both sides'
# lines (ours first) instead of writing conflict markers. Caveats, all handled by `kbgit.py fix`:
# - a row or line that BOTH sides edited is kept twice (old and new version): fix merges rows by id;
# - the same new line added on both sides can appear twice: fix drops exact duplicates;
# - the CSV header is shared context and is not duplicated; a header changed on one side needs a human;
# - union works on lines: CSV records must stay on one line (no newline inside a quoted field), and every
#   file must end with a newline (the tools write both that way);
# - two answers added under the same QK id end up under one heading: review; ids from different questions differ;
# - union never fails, so it cannot tell a real disagreement from an addition: read the merged diff.
_sources.csv merge=union
_fetch_state.csv merge=union
_answers.md merge=union
_gaps.md merge=union
_conflicts.md merge=union

# Append-style tool data (one record per line): parallel additions keep both sides' rows. fix does not clean them:
# a row both sides edited stays twice, which tests.py reports (duplicate signal or alias term) or `rag.py eval` shows
# (a doubled lookup_eval.csv id); keep the newer row. Orphaned doc2query keys: `python3 _tools/doc2query.py stale`.
kb/*/_retrieval/doc2query/expansions.csv merge=union
kb/*/_retrieval/signals.csv merge=union
_tools/aliases.csv merge=union
kb/*/_retrieval/lookup_eval.csv merge=union

# Generated files: union so a merge never stops on them, then `kbgit.py fix` regenerates them
# (build_index.py for _coverage.csv; lint_baseline.txt = current lint errors that either side had accepted).
# _self/coverage.md is NOT here: its header is hand-written and it merges normally; if its generated coverage table
# conflicts, `kbgit.py fix` rewrites the table (markers inside it included); `git add _self/coverage.md` afterwards.
_coverage.csv merge=union
_tools/lint_baseline.txt merge=union

